[{:seon.task/name "one-monotonic-clock"
  :seon.task/plan "Done; what exists. Seon has one monotonic clock: `seon.profile/now-ns`, uncontracted (seon.profile is not metered), a Chez foreign read of clock_gettime_nsec_np(CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW), the clock every process shares (a forked child reads the same timeline): 9.5 ns a read, no allocation, 41 ns resolution on the M1, against Jolt's System/nanoTime at 102 ns and 225 bytes through its contract. `seon.profile/ms-since` is the one 'ms since t0' (it lives in seon.profile because seon.contract and seon.db cannot require seon.lib). `seon.lib/now-ns`, `seon.sock/now-ns` and every System/nanoTime in src and test are gone; the profiler's samples are no longer quantized to 1 us. v1: System/nanoTime on the JVM, one clock within one process. Wiki: docs/machine.md#the-clock."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.profile-test/the-clock-is-the-one-every-process-reads seon.profile-test/reading-the-clock-allocates-nothing seon.profile-test/every-touched-function-has-an-estimated-time}}]
